WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.269
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C two, Book three.

2
00:00:05.569 --> 00:00:09.373
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.673 --> 00:00:15.100
Unit one: Complexity Science. Today you will explain order using result-of language.

4
00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:18.018
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:18.967 --> 00:00:22.049
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:22.883 --> 00:00:24.778
Complex adaptive system.

7
00:00:25.783 --> 00:00:31.254
Complex adaptive system means a network of agents that learn and change in response to one another.

8
00:00:31.850 --> 00:00:34.720
A rainforest is a complex adaptive system.

9
00:00:35.317 --> 00:00:38.817
Your turn. Say: Complex adaptive system.

10
00:00:41.667 --> 00:00:43.527
Complex adaptive system.

11
00:00:44.283 --> 00:00:45.366
Emergence.

12
00:00:46.350 --> 00:00:50.056
Emergence means new patterns arising from simple interactions.

13
00:00:50.650 --> 00:00:53.207
The flock's shape is an example of emergence.

14
00:00:53.817 --> 00:00:56.295
Your turn. Say: Emergence.

15
00:00:59.133 --> 00:01:00.297
Emergence.

16
00:01:01.050 --> 00:01:02.515
Self-organization.

17
00:01:03.517 --> 00:01:07.026
Self-organization means order forming without a central controller.

18
00:01:07.617 --> 00:01:11.207
Self-organization explains how termites build great mounds.

19
00:01:11.817 --> 00:01:15.108
Your turn. Say: Self-organization.

20
00:01:17.967 --> 00:01:19.560
Self-organization.

21
00:01:20.317 --> 00:01:22.038
Nonlinear dynamics.

22
00:01:23.050 --> 00:01:27.685
Nonlinear dynamics means behaviour in which small changes can have large effects.

23
00:01:28.283 --> 00:01:32.640
Nonlinear dynamics make long-range weather forecasts unreliable.

24
00:01:33.250 --> 00:01:36.715
Your turn. Say: Nonlinear dynamics.

25
00:01:39.567 --> 00:01:41.241
Nonlinear dynamics.

26
00:01:41.983 --> 00:01:43.054
Attractor.

27
00:01:44.050 --> 00:01:47.977
Attractor means a stable state toward which a system tends to settle.

28
00:01:48.583 --> 00:01:51.674
The economy returned to its old attractor after the shock.

29
00:01:52.283 --> 00:01:54.854
Your turn. Say: Attractor.

30
00:01:57.700 --> 00:01:58.678
Attractor.

31
00:01:59.417 --> 00:02:00.476
Feedback.

32
00:02:01.483 --> 00:02:05.364
Feedback means information about results that changes the next action.

33
00:02:05.950 --> 00:02:08.844
Positive feedback accelerated the collapse.

34
00:02:09.450 --> 00:02:11.998
Your turn. Say: Feedback.

35
00:02:14.833 --> 00:02:15.846
Feedback.

36
00:02:16.583 --> 00:02:17.747
Resilience.

37
00:02:18.750 --> 00:02:22.247
Resilience means the capacity to absorb shocks and recover.

38
00:02:22.850 --> 00:02:25.593
Diverse crops give a farm greater resilience.

39
00:02:26.183 --> 00:02:29.300
Your turn. Say: Resilience.

40
00:02:32.167 --> 00:02:33.446
Resilience.

41
00:02:34.183 --> 00:02:35.498
Phase transition.

42
00:02:36.483 --> 00:02:40.724
Phase transition means a sudden change from one state of a system to another.

43
00:02:41.317 --> 00:02:43.827
Water freezing is a classic phase transition.

44
00:02:44.417 --> 00:02:47.499
Your turn. Say: Phase transition.

45
00:02:50.333 --> 00:02:51.741
Phase transition.

46
00:02:52.533 --> 00:02:56.556
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:57.417 --> 00:03:01.460
Visal, how can a flock of birds move so perfectly with no leader?

48
00:03:02.550 --> 00:03:12.981
Order often emerges from apparent chaos. Each bird follows simple local rules, and the pattern arises from those interactions.

49
00:03:14.083 --> 00:03:15.909
So nobody designs the pattern?

50
00:03:17.017 --> 00:03:24.058
Correct. Self-organization results from feedback between neighbours, not from central command.

51
00:03:25.150 --> 00:03:27.126
Can such systems suddenly break?

52
00:03:28.217 --> 00:03:35.664
Yes. A phase transition can stem from a tiny change, which is why resilience matters so much.

53
00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:38.149
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:38.983 --> 00:03:43.398
Grammar focus: Result-of language (arises from, emerges from).

55
00:03:43.698 --> 00:03:44.803
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:45.517 --> 00:03:48.085
Order often emerges from apparent chaos.

57
00:03:48.983 --> 00:03:52.330
Self-organization results from feedback between neighbours.

58
00:03:53.217 --> 00:03:56.412
A phase transition can stem from a tiny change.

59
00:03:57.367 --> 00:04:05.476
Link an outcome to its source with arise from, emerge from, result from or stem from. The preposition is always 'from': order emerges from chaos.

60
00:04:06.217 --> 00:04:10.030
Be careful! Don't say: Order emerges of chaos.

61
00:04:11.967 --> 00:04:14.396
Say: Order emerges from chaos.

62
00:04:14.696 --> 00:04:16.068
emerge from (not 'of').

63
00:04:16.867 --> 00:04:19.600
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:21.900
Order Without a Ruler.

65
00:04:22.200 --> 00:04:26.870
Complexity science studies systems whose behaviour cannot be predicted from their parts alone.

66
00:04:27.867 --> 00:04:34.603
A flock, an ecosystem and a market are each a complex adaptive system, in which many agents adjust to one another.

67
00:04:35.600 --> 00:04:42.232
Order often emerges from apparent chaos, because simple local rules and feedback produce large-scale patterns.

68
00:04:43.233 --> 00:04:49.076
Nonlinear dynamics mean that a small cause can trigger a phase transition, so prediction is limited.

69
00:04:50.067 --> 00:04:53.227
Resilience, not control, is therefore the realistic goal.

70
00:04:54.333 --> 00:04:58.590
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:59.450 --> 00:05:01.717
Order emerges blank apparent chaos.

72
00:05:02.017 --> 00:05:05.322
A, from. B, of. C, by.

73
00:05:08.867 --> 00:05:10.518
The answer is A: from.

74
00:05:11.683 --> 00:05:14.635
Order forming without a central controller is blank.

75
00:05:14.935 --> 00:05:19.064
A, attractor. B, self-organization. C, feedback.

76
00:05:22.600 --> 00:05:25.285
The answer is B: self-organization.

77
00:05:26.450 --> 00:05:29.552
A sudden change from one state to another is a blank.

78
00:05:29.852 --> 00:05:34.133
A, phase transition. B, resilience. C, emergence.

79
00:05:37.667 --> 00:05:39.875
The answer is A: phase transition.

80
00:05:41.017 --> 00:05:42.633
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:05:42.933 --> 00:05:49.640
A, Order results of local rules.. B, Order results from local rules.. C, Order results by local rules..

82
00:05:53.200 --> 00:05:56.906
The answer is B: Order results from local rules..

83
00:05:58.050 --> 00:06:01.269
According to the reading, the realistic goal is…

84
00:06:01.569 --> 00:06:05.721
A, total control. B, resilience. C, prediction.

85
00:06:09.267 --> 00:06:11.475
The answer is B: resilience.

86
00:06:12.667 --> 00:06:15.865
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:16.717 --> 00:06:19.993
Can you think of a system in nature that exhibits self-organization?

88
00:06:36.783 --> 00:06:38.272
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:38.572 --> 00:07:03.381
An ant colony is a good example. No ant gives orders, yet trails, nests and food storage emerge from simple local rules, such as following a chemical scent. The colony adapts when food runs out, because feedback strengthens successful trails. Its intelligence arises from interaction, not from any single leader.

90
00:07:03.681 --> 00:07:05.530
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:06.417 --> 00:07:12.099
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and result-of language (arises from, emerges from).

92
00:07:12.399 --> 00:07:16.851
Today's value is systems thinking. See how the parts connect to make the whole.

93
00:07:17.151 --> 00:07:19.525
See you in Unit two. Goodbye!

94
00:07:19.825 --> 00:07:22.663
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

